  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/45e8ddd8ddb33dc8d6a701ed75878271b331ce6b3591ba8fae9f4fa49020c12b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
